[2026-09-19 05:45:44 -05:00] DEEP READ BLOCK 1 — WHI / hexclass / early scheduler intent
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Source roots: D:\pg\ai\hexclass.txt, modfiletest.txt (T0 classify codes),
  nova-ai\awareness\data\nova.devlog, agents Gx Modelfile WHI tables.

hexclass.txt (48 entries)
  Shape: keys like 1x0F000000000 / 1x17000000000 — Dewey-inspired hex taxonomy.
  Observed coverage so far: 1x0* Generalities (16), 1x1* Philosophy/Psychology (16),
  1x2* Religion (16). File ends at 1x2F — incomplete encyclopedia; pattern is
  clear: fixed-width hex class codes for instantaneous bucket recognition later.
  T0 Modelfile already expects classify output like "1x03 0001" — early WHI use.
  Lesson: do NOT require LLM to invent taxonomy; stamp known codes, train later
  models to emit/recognize them for pattern match without full RAG.

Gx Modelfile (dormant wing)
  Parallel WHI family: Gx1000-helmet … Gx9000-meta. Different namespace from 1x.
  Fieldkit README already says Gx dormant — keep civil homestead on Ax/Tx/0x first.

nova.devlog (Jan 2026)
  Early correct instinct: background Python scheduler as agency spine; modular
  task manager; hardware limits as first-class (id/ego framing = metal constraint).
  Fieldkit later crystallized this as sched.tick + jobs table — that was the win.
  Mistake risk if we forget: early logs fantasized microservices; fieldkit rightly
  stayed one SQLite palace on Windows metal.

Homestead evolution note
  WHI index is the long game for pattern recognition after frontier fine-tunes.
  Near term: keep stamping WHI on packets/facts (chronicler mask); preserve hexclass
  + Gx tables as reference data under palace or STAGING, not as always-on prompt bulk.


